<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Connecticut River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> River is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, flowing south from near the Canadian border to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a>. English settlers founded Hartford, Windsor and Wethersfield on its banks in the 1630s, and Saybrook guarded its mouth. Its upper course became a disputed boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, leading to the Vermont Republic and the Indian Stream dispute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/connecticut-river.jpg" alt="Connecticut River" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: It'sOnlyMakeBelieve · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Connecticut_River_From_Gillette_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
